    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <section class="mb-12">
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">If you ship in 40-foot containers out of Port Klang or Westports — especially bagged goods, beverages, or consumer-packaged products — you have probably wondered whether the pallets you ship with are eating freight cost you don't need to pay. They are. Slip sheets are the alternative the largest exporters in Malaysia have been using for decades, and the maths is straightforward once you understand how they work and what equipment is involved. Here is the breakdown for warehouse and operations managers in Klang Valley.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">What a Slip Sheet Actually Is</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Construction & Specification</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">A slip sheet is a 1.5-3mm fibreboard sheet — solid, multi-ply paper bonded into a tough laminate. One or more edges fold up to form a 5-8cm lip that the push-pull attachment grips. Standard sizes match common pallet footprints (1100×1100mm for Asian markets, 1200×1000mm for European, 1219×1016mm for North American). Plastic slip sheets exist for hygiene-critical and high-moisture applications, but paper is dominant.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">How the Push-Pull Process Works</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Goods stack on the slip sheet just like a pallet. To load, the forklift's push-pull attachment grips the lip with a clamp, pulls the entire load onto its platen, and the operator drives the load to its destination. To unload, push the platen forward and the goods slide off onto the receiving floor with the slip sheet underneath. Total cycle time per load matches a pallet — once operators are trained.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Where the Slip Sheet Goes</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">The slip sheet stays with the goods through the freight journey, then either disposes into the destination's paper-recycling stream or returns in some closed-loop operations. For exporters, that means no return-pallet logistics, no pallet pool fees, and no quarantine treatment costs.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">The Economics</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Per-Unit Cost</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Slip sheet RM 8-15 vs pallet RM 25-60 — straight per-unit saving of RM 17-45. Across 10,000 units shipped per year, that's RM 170,000-450,000 directly off the operating cost. Even after accounting for the push-pull attachment investment (one-time RM 25,000-45,000), payback typically lands within 6-12 months for medium-volume exporters.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Container Space Savings</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">A pallet is 145mm tall — that's 145mm of vertical space taken from product. Stripping the pallet means an extra row of cartons fits in the same container. For a 40-foot container shipped weekly to a single destination, that's 50+ extra cartons per shipment, 2,500+ per year — billable freight you collect or freight you no longer pay.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">No ISPM-15 Treatment</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Slip sheets are paper-based — exempt from the ISPM-15 fumigation requirement that all wooden export packaging must satisfy. RM 25-50 per pallet saved on heat-treatment fees, plus the risk of quarantine hold disappears. For exporters to fumigation-strict markets (EU, US, Australia, China), that alone justifies the switch.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">When Pallets Still Win</h2>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Slip sheets are not universal. Short-haul trucking with frequent forklift handling at both ends — pallets win on speed. Closed-loop pool operations like CHEP — pallets win because the asset returns. Rack-stored inventory at destination — pallets win because slip-sheeted loads cannot rack. Tall-stack fragile goods that would topple without pallet stability — pallets win. Read your operation lane by lane before committing.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="bg-surface-variant/50 border border-outline-variant/20 rounded-lg p-8 my-12">
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Slip Sheet vs Pallet at a Glance</h3>
